{
  "A": "Research a serial bytewise MSD radix sort to replace quadratic insertion sorting. Sort indices into the original strings, using unsigned-byte order, a terminal NUL bucket that is never recursed into, actual histogram-derived offsets, and reusable O(N) scratch storage. Preserve strcmp ordering, duplicate multiplicity, and original token spellings. Explore bounded small-partition insertion cutoffs and common-prefix skipping; avoid reading past terminators or assuming zero-filled padding. Handle skewed distributions without fixed-capacity bucket slices. Keep I/O changes minimal to isolate sorting gains, and report standalone correctness, timing, and memory costs through the unchanged workflow.",
  "B": "Develop an independent comparison-sort alternative with guaranteed O(N log N) worst-case behavior, such as mergesort or introsort with a bounded fallback. Preserve exact strcmp semantics while sorting indices rather than repeatedly moving strings. Explore up to four workers within the fixed build/runtime permissions: balanced input chunks followed by merges avoid dependence on first-character bucket balance. Compare against a serial version and measure thread-startup, scratch-memory, and merge overhead; parallelism may lose on small inputs. Use only general, input-derived thresholds, and remove equal-occupancy assumptions.",
  "C": "Research input/output throughput independently of the sorting strategy. Replace per-token fscanf and printf calls with buffered fread tokenization and buffered fwrite output. Support non-seekable stdin, ordinary whitespace, tokens spanning buffer boundaries, and a final token without trailing whitespace. Parse all four header fields correctly, preserve leading zeroes and every token byte, and emit exactly one newline per sorted token. Use checked, appropriately sized storage and indices. Hold a correct, distribution-safe sorting implementation fixed when attributing gains, and provide clean interfaces for integration with either A or B.",
  "G": "Integrate measured programs, not presumed additive improvements. Retain standalone winners and compare A, B, C, A+C, and B+C through the unchanged correctness/timing workflow and existing budget. Initially treat A and B as competing sorting engines; attempt parallel-radix or other hybrids only when measurements justify the added complexity. Select by repeated end-to-end performance, including parsing, allocation, worker startup, sorting, and output. Audit bytewise ordering, leading zeroes, duplicates, terminators, skewed occupancy, and index/allocation arithmetic. Preserve the best validated standalone candidate whenever integration regresses. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions, or budgets.",
  "analysis": "The measured baseline is correct on four cases and averages 0.417456 seconds over three runs; no candidate measurements or phase breakdown establish the dominant bottleneck. Source inspection suggests two strong hypotheses: quadratic per-bucket insertion sorting and formatted-I/O overhead. Equal-sized bucket slices combined with un-compacted output can also overlap or expose unwritten entries under uneven occupancy, so replacements must use actual counts or global sorting. Preserve the reference's strcmp-based string semantics rather than inferring numeric order or normalizing leading zeroes. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU; multicore benefits, algorithm choices, and integration gains all require measurement."
}